<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babemba Traoré</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ba Bemba Traoré</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babemba Traoré was the last king of Kénédougou, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> from 1893. He succeeded his brother Tieba, who had allied with the French, but refused French demands to accept a garrison. When a French column stormed Sikasso on 1 May 1898 after a long siege, Babemba chose death rather than capture, and his kingdom was absorbed into French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Babemba was the fifth and last Faama of the Kénédougou Kingdom. On taking the throne he faced both the expanding Wassoulou Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a> and a French colonial army that was conquering what remained of the Toucouleur Empire. He kept up the fairly close relationship his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tieba-traore">Tieba Traoré</a> had built with the French, and extended Kénédougou's territory into what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>.</p><p>By 1898 the French had conquered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a>'s empire and turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a>. Colonel Audeoud, acting French commander of French Sudan, was looking for a new victory to further his reputation, and sent a commission to Sikasso to set up a French garrison there. Babemba refused this humiliation outright. He expelled the commission and stopped paying the yearly tribute, and according to French sources his troops attacked the commission on its way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The French answered with an invasion and on 15 April 1898 opened a heavy artillery bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a>'s walls. The defenders made fierce sallies but could not drive the besiegers away, and the city fell on 1 May amid furious fighting from house to house. Babemba, wounded while defending the citadel, ordered his bodyguards to kill him. Sikasso was sacked, and 4,000 captives were taken and shared out as slaves among the victorious soldiers, both the conscripted Africans and the Europeans.</p><p>Babemba is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> as a hero of resistance to colonial rule, and his order to his bodyguards is honoured there. The stadium of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a>, Stade Babemba, bears his name, and the city has statues of him and of his brother Tieba.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Babemba Traoré” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His brother <b>Babemba Traore</b> refused French demands, and on 1 May 1898 a French column stormed the city; Babemba died rather than surrender.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenedougou-kingdom">Kenedougou Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.32,-5.67&amp;year=1888" aria-label="Sikasso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Babemba Traoré"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4526 4038h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4526 4038h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=11.32,-5.67&amp;year=1888">What was here in 1888? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
